window.INTERVIEW_EXECUTION["swift-28"] = {"timeBudget": {"navigation": 4, "solution": 9, "coding": 26, "verification": 6}, "navigation": {"say": "This is a subsequence question, not a window question, so nothing here is about consecutive builds. I want to settle three things before I write: what improving means when two scores are equal, whether the caller needs the builds or only the count, and whether the later parts are the same question or new ones.", "questions": ["Is improving strict, so two builds with the same score cannot both be in a chain?", "When several chains tie for longest, does it matter which one I report?", "Does one envelope nest inside another when only one of its two limits is smaller?"], "assumptions": ["Improving is strict everywhere: equal scores never chain, and equal limits never nest.", "Among equally long chains I report a documented one, resolving ties toward the earliest predecessor and the earliest ending build, so the answer is reproducible.", "Both limits must be strictly smaller for one envelope to nest inside another.", "An empty history has no chain, which is zero for the length and a typed failure for the peak question, since a peak of nothing is not meaningful."], "edgeCases": ["an empty history, and a history of one build", "a history whose scores are all equal, which is the strictness test", "a strictly rising and a strictly falling history, which are the two peak failures", "envelopes sharing a CPU limit, which is where the sort comparator gets decided", "a history whose best chain does not end at the last build"], "restate": "Given benchmark scores per build, report the longest strictly improving chain's length and the builds in it, then the same length by the log-linear route, then the deepest nesting of two-dimensional envelopes and the fewest builds to drop for a single-peak shape."}, "solution": {"pitch": "One entry per build, meaning the best improving chain that ends exactly there, plus a recorded predecessor. That answers the first two parts. The third part throws the table away for one sorted array of best chain endings, which is the log-linear formulation, and the fourth part is that same scan applied twice: once to a sorted second dimension, once in each direction.", "dataStructures": ["A length array and a predecessor array of the same size, filled together so the reconstruction never has to search.", "One sorted array of best endings for the fast scan, where position L holds the smallest score that can end a chain of length L plus one.", "A per-position length array falling out of the same fast scan, which is what makes the peak part two calls rather than two implementations."], "types": ["BuildResult pairs the identifier with the score, so the reconstruction can name builds without a parallel lookup.", "ResourceEnvelope is a struct rather than a tuple so tests can compare envelopes directly and the two limits are named at the point of comparison.", "ChainError separates an empty history from a history that simply has no peak, because those are different things for a caller to handle."], "composition": "The naming method is the primitive and the length method is its count, so the two can never disagree. The fast method is deliberately not built on either: it is checked against the quadratic one as an invariant on every fixture, which is a stronger statement than a call would be. Both fourth-part methods call the same shared scan the third part introduced.", "tradeoff": "The quadratic table is what makes reconstruction easy, and the fast scan is what makes long histories cheap. Keeping both is the honest answer here rather than a redundancy: reconstructing from the fast scan is possible but needs a second array recording which build occupies each length and a predecessor per build, which is more machinery than a build history of interview scale justifies.", "trap": "Returning the sorted array of best endings as the chain. It has the right length and it is increasing, so it looks exactly right, and it is frequently not a subsequence of the history at all. The other trap is the envelope sort: an ascending secondary key passes every fixture that has no duplicate first limits."}, "coding": {"order": ["Write the quadratic scan with the predecessor array, and finish it by walking the predecessor back into build order.", "Make the length method one line on top of it, and say why rather than filling a second table.", "Write the shared scan next, reporting both the array of best endings and the per-position lengths, and hand-write the halving search inside it.", "The fast length method and the array method are then one line each on top of that scan.", "Sort the envelopes and call the scan; state the secondary key out loud as you write the comparator.", "Finish with the peak: the scan forwards, the scan on the reversed scores, and the combination at each valid position."], "commentary": ["I am defining one entry as the best chain ending exactly at this build, not the best chain seen so far, because only the first of those lets me name the builds afterwards.", "I improve strictly, which decides the tie in favour of the earliest predecessor and is what makes the reported chain reproducible rather than whatever the loop happened to leave behind.", "The answer is the largest entry rather than the last one, because these chains end all over the history.", "This part abandons the table on purpose. The array I keep is not lengths, it is the smallest score that can end a chain of each length, and it stays sorted, which is exactly what lets me halve the search.", "I am writing the halving search out rather than reaching for a linear find, because a linear find returns the same index and puts the quadratic factor straight back.", "The secondary sort key is descending so two envelopes sharing a CPU limit are handed to the scan in an order that cannot chain. Ascending here is the classic bug and it is invisible without duplicate limits.", "The peak is counted by both directions, so I subtract one, and I only consider positions where both sides are non-trivial."], "checkIn": "After the second part, say: parts one and two share one table, and the third part is the same question with a different algorithm rather than a new sub-problem. Do you want me to write the log-linear version now, or talk through why it is correct first?"}, "verification": {"walkthroughs": ["Walk the worked scores ten, nine, twelve, eleven, fourteen: the per-build lengths come out one, one, two, two, three, so the answer is three and the best chain ends at the last build.", "Walk the predecessors back from that build: it came through the third build, which came through the first, giving the first, third and fifth builds in order.", "Walk the fast scan over the same scores: ten starts the array, nine overwrites it, twelve extends it, eleven overwrites the twelve, and fourteen extends it, leaving three entries and therefore the same answer of three.", "Walk the peak on those scores: the rise lengths are one, one, two, two, three and the fall lengths are two, one, two, one, one, so the only build with both sides non-trivial is the third, keeping three builds and dropping two.", "Walk the envelope sort on a fixture where every CPU limit is equal: the descending secondary key hands the scan a falling sequence, which chains nowhere, which is the right answer of one."], "tests": "Run swift test --filter Problem28BuildRegressionChainAnalyzerTests and read the four suites in part order. The all-equal fixture in the first suite is what catches a non-strict comparison, the pseudo-random fixture in the third is what catches an append that should be an overwrite, and the shared-CPU fixture in the fourth is what catches an ascending secondary sort key.", "complexity": "The table is quadratic in the number of builds with linear space, and the scan is log-linear with linear space. Be honest that no test here proves the log factor: a linear find inside the scan produces identical output and passes everything, so the halving search is a requirement of the problem rather than something the suite can enforce. The envelope part adds the log-linear sort, and the peak part is two passes of the scan.", "moreTime": "Counting how many chains achieve the best length needs a second array carried alongside the lengths, resetting on an improvement and accumulating on a tie, and it does not survive the log-linear reformulation without an indexed tree. Reconstructing the chain from the fast scan needs the build occupying each length recorded as the scan runs. A version that maximises the total score rather than the number of builds is a good question to raise precisely because the fast scan stops working: its greedy invariant is about length, not value. Bounding how far apart two chained scores may be needs a max query over a value range, which is a tree rather than a variation on the recurrence."}, "code": "/// One benchmark run: a build identifier and the score that build recorded.\npublic struct BuildResult: Equatable, Sendable {\n    public let id: String\n    public let score: Int\n\n    public init(id: String, score: Int) {\n        self.id = id\n        self.score = score\n    }\n}\n\n/// The container limits a build ran under. One envelope fits strictly inside\n/// another only when both of its limits are strictly smaller.\npublic struct ResourceEnvelope: Equatable, Sendable {\n    public let cpuMillicores: Int\n    public let memoryMebibytes: Int\n\n    public init(cpuMillicores: Int, memoryMebibytes: Int) {\n        self.cpuMillicores = cpuMillicores\n        self.memoryMebibytes = memoryMebibytes\n    }\n}\n\npublic enum ChainError: Error, Equatable, Sendable {\n    case emptyHistory\n    case noValidPeak\n    case notImplemented\n}\n\npublic struct ChainAnalyzer: Sendable {\n    public init() {}\n\n    // MARK: Part 1 - Longest improving chain length\n\n    /// How many builds are in the longest strictly improving chain.\n    ///\n    /// The chain itself is the primitive and this is its length, so the two can\n    /// never disagree about what the longest chain is. Filling a second table\n    /// that only produces the number would be the same work twice.\n    public func longestImprovingChainLength(_ builds: [BuildResult]) -> Int {\n        longestImprovingChain(builds).count\n    }\n\n    // MARK: Part 2 - Name the builds in the chain\n\n    /// The identifiers of the longest strictly improving chain, in build order.\n    ///\n    /// One quadratic table plus one predecessor array. `best[i]` is the length of\n    /// the longest improving chain that ends exactly at build `i`, which is what\n    /// makes the entries comparable: every chain has to end somewhere, so the\n    /// answer is the largest entry rather than the last one.\n    ///\n    /// Both tie-breaks are deliberate and both are what make the returned list\n    /// deterministic: among equally good predecessors the earliest wins, and\n    /// among equally long chains the one ending earliest wins.\n    public func longestImprovingChain(_ builds: [BuildResult]) -> [String] {\n        guard !builds.isEmpty else { return [] }\n\n        var best = Array(repeating: 1, count: builds.count)\n        var predecessor = Array(repeating: -1, count: builds.count)\n\n        for index in builds.indices {\n            for earlier in 0..<index where builds[earlier].score < builds[index].score {\n                // Strictly greater, so the earliest qualifying predecessor is the\n                // one that survives a tie.\n                if best[earlier] + 1 > best[index] {\n                    best[index] = best[earlier] + 1\n                    predecessor[index] = earlier\n                }\n            }\n        }\n\n        var endIndex = 0\n        for index in builds.indices where best[index] > best[endIndex] {\n            endIndex = index\n        }\n\n        var chain: [String] = []\n        var cursor = endIndex\n        while cursor >= 0 {\n            chain.append(builds[cursor].id)\n            cursor = predecessor[cursor]\n        }\n        return chain.reversed()\n    }\n\n    // MARK: Part 3 - Log-linear chain length\n\n    /// The same number as Part 1, computed without the quadratic table.\n    ///\n    /// This is the one part that does not reuse an earlier part, and that is the\n    /// lesson: the fast formulation abandons the table entirely. What it keeps\n    /// instead is one sorted array whose length is the answer.\n    public func longestImprovingChainLengthFast(_ builds: [BuildResult]) -> Int {\n        tailsScan(builds.map(\\.score)).tails.count\n    }\n\n    /// The tails array left behind by that scan.\n    ///\n    /// Entry `L` is the smallest value that can end an improving chain of length\n    /// `L + 1`. The array is always sorted, which is exactly what licenses the\n    /// binary search, and it is **not** in general a chain from the input:\n    /// returning it as the answer to Part 2 is the confident wrong answer this\n    /// method exists to make visible.\n    public func improvingChainTails(_ builds: [BuildResult]) -> [Int] {\n        tailsScan(builds.map(\\.score)).tails\n    }\n\n    // MARK: Part 4 - Nested envelopes and peak-shaped trains\n\n    /// The deepest run of envelopes that each fit strictly inside the next.\n    ///\n    /// Sorting by CPU ascending reduces one of the two dimensions to input order,\n    /// leaving an improving-chain question on memory. The secondary key is\n    /// **descending** on purpose: two envelopes with the same CPU must never\n    /// chain, and putting the larger memory first is what makes the improving\n    /// scan skip over them. An ascending secondary key looks identical and\n    /// silently reports nesting that does not exist.\n    public func deepestEnvelopeNesting(_ envelopes: [ResourceEnvelope]) -> Int {\n        let ordered = envelopes.sorted {\n            $0.cpuMillicores != $1.cpuMillicores\n                ? $0.cpuMillicores < $1.cpuMillicores\n                : $0.memoryMebibytes > $1.memoryMebibytes\n        }\n        return tailsScan(ordered.map(\\.memoryMebibytes)).tails.count\n    }\n\n    /// The fewest builds to drop so the remaining scores rise to one peak and\n    /// then fall.\n    ///\n    /// Run the Part 3 scan forwards for the longest improving chain ending at\n    /// each build, then run it over the reversed scores for the longest chain\n    /// starting at each build and falling away from it. A build can be the peak\n    /// only when both sides have something on them, so both lengths must be at\n    /// least two; the peak itself is counted twice, hence the subtraction.\n    public func minimumRemovalsForPeakShape(_ builds: [BuildResult]) throws(ChainError) -> Int {\n        guard !builds.isEmpty else { throw .emptyHistory }\n\n        let scores = builds.map(\\.score)\n        let rising = tailsScan(scores).lengthEndingAt\n        let falling = Array(tailsScan(scores.reversed()).lengthEndingAt.reversed())\n\n        var bestKept = 0\n        for index in builds.indices where rising[index] >= 2 && falling[index] >= 2 {\n            bestKept = max(bestKept, rising[index] + falling[index] - 1)\n        }\n        guard bestKept > 0 else { throw .noValidPeak }\n        return builds.count - bestKept\n    }\n\n    // MARK: Shared machinery\n\n    /// The patience scan, run once and reporting both of the things Parts 3 and 4\n    /// need from it: the final tails array, and the length of the longest\n    /// improving chain ending at each position.\n    ///\n    /// The second falls straight out of the first: a value placed at tails index\n    /// `p` ends a chain of length `p + 1`, because tails is sorted and every\n    /// earlier slot holds a value it could sit on top of.\n    private func tailsScan(_ values: [Int]) -> (tails: [Int], lengthEndingAt: [Int]) {\n        var tails: [Int] = []\n        var lengthEndingAt = Array(repeating: 0, count: values.count)\n\n        for index in values.indices {\n            let value = values[index]\n            let position = lowerBound(tails, value)\n            if position == tails.count {\n                tails.append(value)\n            } else {\n                tails[position] = value\n            }\n            lengthEndingAt[index] = position + 1\n        }\n\n        return (tails, lengthEndingAt)\n    }\n\n    /// The first index of `sorted` holding a value greater than or equal to\n    /// `value`, found by halving rather than scanning.\n    ///\n    /// A linear search would return the same index and would turn the whole scan\n    /// back into quadratic work while still passing every test. The comparison is\n    /// the strictness knob: `<` here keeps chains strictly improving, and `<=`\n    /// would allow equal scores to chain.\n    private func lowerBound(_ sorted: [Int], _ value: Int) -> Int {\n        var low = 0\n        var high = sorted.count\n        while low < high {\n            let middle = low + (high - low) / 2\n            if sorted[middle] < value {\n                low = middle + 1\n            } else {\n                high = middle\n            }\n        }\n        return low\n    }\n}\n"};
